{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "Let D(s) be the diagonal biconditional s ↔ K(F(∼s)). The source chooses s so that T proves K(D(s)); Lean requires an s such that the base proves D(s) itself. Base necessitation and inclusion give the source's boxed theorem from the Lean hypothesis, but not conversely: a proof of K(D(s)) in the extension need not give an unboxed proof in the base. An arithmetic diagonal lemma can justify the stronger hypothesis in a concrete application, but it is a materially stronger fixed-point requirement in the abstract statement."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The source defines Con q as a specific universally quantified arithmetic sentence. Lean allows any conF from which the base can prove each sentence-code instance separately. This suffices when instantiated with the intended Con q, but neither identifies conF with that sentence nor expresses the universal condition over numbers."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "In the declared abstraction, F need not come from a single one-free-variable formula q(x), and F(∼s) uses sentence negation directly rather than an arithmetic term ng acting on a code. Lean thus does not express the syntactic setting in which diagonalization supplies the fixed point."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Lean packages the background axioms and their boxed forms using a separate base closed under necessitation and a theorem-inclusion hypothesis. The source states the result for T against its specified axiomatic background, not as a theorem about a pair of abstract systems."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The sentencewise upper-bound hypothesis, the conclusion K(∼K conF), and the use of boxed rather than plain correctness match the source. The main mismatch is the requirement of an unboxed base proof where the source's proof specifies a boxed theorem of T."
}
